---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
---

<Page
  title="Features — Open Note"
  description="Everything Open Note does: a Markdown editor that decorates real source, autocomplete for links, tags and emoji, Git sync that never resolves a conflict for you, a note list, tags and search, callouts and maths, export to HTML, DOCX and Textbundle, a URL scheme and a CLI — and a fully rebindable keymap."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom: 1rem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3rem)">Everything it does</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In detail, and with the reasoning — because most of these are decisions, not just features.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section plain">
    <div class="wrap">
      <!-- Editor -->
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>An editor over real Markdown</h3>
          <p>
            The text in the window <em>is</em> the file. Open Note decorates it in place — headings
            grow, bold goes bold, syntax markers fade — but it never converts your note into a
            document model and back again.
          </p>
          <p>
            That matters for two reasons. A round-trip through a rich-text model quietly rewrites
            formatting it does not understand, and it turns every edit into a noisy diff. Here a
            one-word change is a one-word change in Git.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>The line you are on shows its source.</strong> Move the caret onto a heading and
              the <code>#</code> comes back, ready to edit. Every other line reads as prose.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Autocomplete for the things you link.</strong> Type <code>[[</code> for note
              titles ranked by recency, <code>#</code> for tags, <code>:</code> for emoji — and a
              title no note has yet stays typeable, so writing the link still creates the note.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The verbs a Markdown editor needs.</strong> Bulleted and numbered lists,
              quotes, code fences, headings one through six, move a line up or down, indent a whole
              list item. Insert today's date or the time in a keystroke.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Tables you can actually edit.</strong> Add or move a row or column, change a
              column's alignment — and the pipes are re-padded so the source stays readable in any
              other editor.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Find and replace</strong> inside the open note, and a full set of shortcuts you
              can rebind.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/editor.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="A note open in the editor with concealed Markdown syntax, a wikilink and a task list."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Sync -->
      <div class="feature flip">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>Git sync you do not have to think about</h3>
          <p>
            Four independent loops run per vault: write, commit, push and fetch. Each is separately
            configurable and separately switchable, and the current state is always on screen —
            <em>synced</em>, <em>committing</em>, <em>pushing</em>, <em>updates available</em>,
            <em>offline</em>, <em>conflict</em>.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Writes</strong> land half a second after you stop typing.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Commits</strong> batch up — thirty seconds idle, or five minutes elapsed — so
              your history is readable instead of one commit per keystroke.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Pushes</strong> follow a commit, and back off when the network is out. Never forced.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Fetches</strong> run on a timer while the window is focused. A clean worktree
              fast-forwards silently and the open note reloads underneath you.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            It runs <strong>your own <code>git</code></strong> rather than an embedded library, so
            SSH agents, credential helpers, GPG signing, corporate proxies and <code>git-lfs</code>
            work because they already work on your machine.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/sync.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="The sync settings panel, showing toggles and intervals for the commit, push and fetch loops."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Conflicts -->
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>Conflicts are never resolved for you</h3>
          <p>
            Because a note is one file, true conflicts are rare — but rare is not never, and one
            silently clobbered note ends any trust in a notes app permanently.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>All automation halts.</strong> Nothing commits, pushes or pulls while a conflict stands.</li>
            <li><strong>The affected notes are named</strong> and badged in the sidebar.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>You choose:</strong> keep yours, keep theirs, or open the file with Git's
              markers intact and merge by hand.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A claimed resolution is verified.</strong> If Git still reports unmerged paths,
              the app does not believe you and stays put.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>There is no auto-merge, no auto-discard and no force push. Anywhere. Ever.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/conflict.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="The conflict screen listing a conflicted note with keep-mine, keep-theirs and manual merge options."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Links & tags -->
      <div class="feature flip">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>Links, backlinks and tags</h3>
          <p>
            Write <code>[[Another note]]</code> and it becomes clickable. Follow a link that has no
            note behind it yet and the note is created — writing the link is how you say it should
            exist.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Backlinks</strong> for the open note, plus <strong>unlinked mentions</strong> —
              the notes that name this one without linking to it, each a click away from becoming a
              real link. That is the link you forgot to make.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Renaming rewrites links.</strong> Rename a note and every
              <code>[[wikilink]]</code> pointing at it is updated in the same commit, so the change
              can be reviewed and reverted as one thing.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Tags are <code>#plain-text</code></strong>, nested with slashes, shown as a
              collapsible tree. Rename or delete a tag across the whole vault in one named commit;
              give one an emoji icon; pin the ones you live in.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Drag a note into a folder</strong> and its links follow it. A move is a rename
              with a different destination.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/links.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="The tag browser open beside a note, listing every tag in the vault with how many notes carry it."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Tasks -->
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>Todos that are still Markdown</h3>
          <p>
            A task is a GFM checkbox. Nothing else is required, and nothing else is stored — so the
            same note renders as a working task list on GitHub.
          </p>
          <pre class="sample"><code>- [ ] Write the docs @sam prio:high due:2026-09-14</code></pre>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Click the checkbox</strong> in the editor to tick it; one character changes on disk.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>One list for the whole vault.</strong> Every open task from every note, with
              the note it came from — click through and land on the exact line.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Optional extras in plain text:</strong> <code>@person</code>,
              <code>prio:high</code>, and <code>due:2026-09-14</code>. Overdue and due-today are
              called out.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Tidy a list:</strong> mark every task done or undone, or move the finished ones
              to the bottom — optionally the moment you tick one.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/tasks.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="The task view with open tasks listed, each showing its source note."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Diagrams -->
      <div class="feature flip">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>Diagrams and drawings</h3>
          <p>
            A fenced <code>mermaid</code> block renders inline as soon as your caret leaves it, and
            goes back to being editable source the moment you return. Maths and callouts, below,
            follow exactly the same rule.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Mermaid renders on GitHub too</strong>, so a diagram in your vault is a diagram
              in your repo's web view.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Graphviz DOT</strong> for graphs that Mermaid does not cover.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Excalidraw</strong> for freehand drawings, stored as plain JSON so they diff
              and merge like anything else — and <code>![[sketch.excalidraw]]</code> renders the
              drawing right in the note, opening the canvas on click.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Rendered SVG is sanitised</strong> — scripts, event handlers and
              <code>javascript:</code> URLs are stripped before anything reaches the screen. A vault
              can be cloned from anywhere.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/diagram.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="A Mermaid flowchart rendered inline beneath its source block in a note."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- History -->
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>History, branches and pull requests</h3>
          <p>
            The whole point of a Git backend is that the history is real. Open Note surfaces it
            without asking you to open a terminal.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Every version of a note</strong>, with the diff, and a one-click restore to any
              of them.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Discard changes</strong> back to the last commit when an edit goes wrong.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Branches</strong> — create, switch, merge and delete. Draft a reorganisation on
              a branch and merge it when it works.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Open a pull request</strong> on GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket for the current
              branch, in your real browser where you are already signed in.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/history.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="The history panel listing commits that touched the open note, with a diff."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Finding things -->
      <div class="feature flip">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>A note list, and finding your way around</h3>
          <p>
            The tree is only one way in. A list pane sits between it and the editor — title, an
            excerpt, the date, an attachment badge — and it is where the app reads as a notes app
            rather than a file browser.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Sort</strong> by modified, title, or created date — created coming from the
              first commit that added the file, the one definition that survives a clone.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Collections:</strong> everything, Today, Untagged, the Archive, or any tag.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Back and forward</strong> across the notes you have opened, restoring where you
              were — a history above the editor, kept separate from undo.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Quick switcher, command palette and full-text search</strong> share one
              overlay; a fourth mode jumps straight to a tag. Layout shortcuts show or hide the tree
              and the list.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/palette.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="The command palette open over a note, listing commands with their keyboard shortcuts."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Everything else in the repo -->
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h3>The rest of the repository</h3>
          <p>
            A vault is an ordinary Git repo, so it holds more than notes — and the app stops
            pretending otherwise.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Text files open properly.</strong> A <code>.txt</code>, a
              <code>config.json</code>, a shell script — all editable, with line numbers and syntax
              highlighting for around 150 languages.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Attach any file</strong> by dropping it in or picking it — images inline, a
              PDF previews in the native viewer, everything else becomes a chip that opens in its OS
              handler. <strong>Reveal in Finder</strong> and <strong>open in the default app</strong>
              are a right-click away.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Read-only notes.</strong> <code>readOnly: true</code> in a note's frontmatter
              locks the editor — and travels with the file, the way app-local state would not.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Multiple vaults</strong> open at once, each with its own sync state, settings
              and branch. Start a new one from a folder of Markdown you already have.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/screenshots/code.png"
            width="1280"
            height="840"
            alt="A TypeScript file open in the plain text editor with line numbers and syntax highlighting."
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Markdown coverage -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Markdown, more of it</h2>
        <p>The ones that earn their place render when someone opens the vault on github.com.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Callouts</strong> — GitHub's <code>&gt; [!NOTE]</code>, <code>[!TIP]</code>,
            <code>[!WARNING]</code> and the rest, drawn with a coloured rule, an icon and a label.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Maths</strong> — <code>$inline$</code> and <code>$$block$$</code> through KaTeX,
            which loads on first use rather than at startup so a vault of prose never pays for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Footnotes</strong> — <code>[^1]</code> concealed to a superscript, the definition
            on hover, and a command to renumber them into document order.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Highlight</strong> (<code>==text==</code>) and <strong>underline</strong>
            (<code>&lt;u&gt;text&lt;/u&gt;</code>) — the underline chosen because it renders
            everywhere, where <code>~text~</code> would collide with strikethrough.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Heading folding</strong> — fold a section down to its heading; a reading posture,
            never written into the file.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Clipboard & export -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Getting notes in and out</h2>
        <p>One converter underneath, Markdown to HTML and back, feeding paste, copy, print and export.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Paste a web page as Markdown</strong> — clean headings and lists instead of a
            wall of HTML — with explicit "paste as plain text", "as raw HTML" and "as a code block"
            when you want them. A URL dropped onto a selection wraps it as a link.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Copy As</strong> plain text, Markdown, HTML, or rich text for an email or a chat
            — optionally with the <code>#tags</code> stripped out.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Print, and print to PDF,</strong> through the system dialog against the same HTML
            the export produces.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Export</strong> a note as self-contained HTML with images inlined and wikilinks
            rewritten to real links, as a <strong>Word document</strong>, or as a
            <strong>Textbundle</strong> that carries its attachments. Export a whole folder at once,
            or <strong>merge</strong> several notes into one.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Appearance -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Make it look right</h2>
        <p>It is a tool for writing prose, so how the prose looks is a setting, not a fixed opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Typography</strong> — the text, heading and code fonts, size, line height, line
            width, paragraph spacing and indent. Applied as CSS variables, so nothing rebuilds and
            the text simply reflows.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Themes</strong> are JSON in <code>.opennote/themes/</code>, so they sync, version
            and can be shared like a note. A handful ship built in, in the same format.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Zoom</strong> the whole editor, and choose whether to conceal Markdown syntax
            everywhere or only off the line you are editing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Spell check is off by default,</strong> deliberately: the OS checker is coupled
            to the smart-quote and dash substitution that turns <code>"key"</code> into curly quotes
            inside a code fence or a YAML block, which quietly corrupts the file.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Info & lifecycle -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>A note's life</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>One info panel</strong> with three tabs: statistics — words, paragraphs, reading
            time, and the created and modified dates — the outline, and the backlinks.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Templates.</strong> A <code>templates/</code> folder, a "new from template"
            command, and <code>{'{{title}}'}</code> / <code>{'{{date}}'}</code> /
            <code>{'{{time}}'}</code> filled in. Daily notes use the same mechanism.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Archive</strong> is a folder, not a hidden flag — moving a note there is visible
            in the tree, on github.com and in the commit. Archived notes drop out of the default list
            and search while staying indexed and linkable.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Automation -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Reach it from outside</h2>
        <p>A URL scheme and a CLI, so a script — or a browser, or a shortcut — can drive it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>An <code>opennote://</code> URL scheme</strong> — open a note, create one, append
            to one, run a search, jump to a tag. Nothing destructive is reachable by URL: append only
            adds, and new refuses to overwrite.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Global hotkeys</strong> to show the window or start a new note, unbound by default
            and set in the same keymap panel as everything else.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A command-line tool</strong> — <code>opennote new</code>, <code>append</code>,
            <code>search</code>, <code>open</code>, reading from stdin where it helps. It works on all
            three desktops and composes with everything a shell can do.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Where things are kept</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Per-vault settings, your keymap and any themes live in <code>.opennote/</code> inside the
          repository, so they travel with the vault between machines. All of it is hand-editable
          JSON, and a broken field falls back to its default rather than taking the app down with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Machine-local state — which vaults you have opened recently, and the zoom level — stays in
          your OS config directory and is never committed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Try it against a repository you already have.</h2>
      <p>Nothing to sign up for, and nothing to migrate.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button big" href="/download">Download Open Note</a>
        <a class="button big ghost" href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
